                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "present_simple",
                    "title": "Present simple: I, you, we, they",
                    "explanation": "El present simple describe rutinas, hábitos y cosas que son verdad en general. Con «I», «you», «we» y «they» el verbo no cambia:\n- «I get up at seven.»\n\nLa negativa es «don't» («do not») + verbo, y la pregunta, «do» + sujeto + verbo:\n- «I don't study.»\n- «Do you eat together? — Yes, we do. / No, we don't.»\n\nCon palabras interrogativas:\n- «What do you do in the evenings?»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Afirmativa",
                        "Negativa",
                        "Pregunta"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "I",
                          "I work",
                          "I don't work",
                          "Do I work?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "you",
                          "you work",
                          "you don't work",
                          "Do you work?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "we",
                          "we work",
                          "we don't work",
                          "Do we work?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "they",
                          "they work",
                          "they don't work",
                          "Do they work?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I get up at seven and I go to work by bus.",
                      "We have dinner together on Fridays.",
                      "I don't study. I play video games!",
                      "What do you do in the evenings?"
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "linking_words",
                    "title": "Linking words: and, but, or, because",
                    "explanation": "Para unir dos ideas en una frase usamos conectores.\n\n«And» (y) añade información:\n- «I study English and I watch TV.»\n\n«But» (pero) presenta un contraste:\n- «I like Bristol, but it rains a lot.»\n\n«Or» (o) da opciones. En negativas también se usa «or»:\n- «Do you go by bus or on foot?»\n- «I don't drink tea or coffee.»\n\n«Because» (porque) da la razón. Para preguntarla usamos «Why…?»:\n- «I go by bus because the office is far.»\n- «Why do you go by bus? — Because the office is far.»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Conector",
                        "Sirve para",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "and",
                          "añadir",
                          "I get up at seven and I go to work."
                        ],
                        [
                          "but",
                          "contrastar",
                          "I love my job, but it's far."
                        ],
                        [
                          "or",
                          "dar opciones",
                          "Tea or coffee?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "because",
                          "explicar la razón",
                          "I don't walk because the office is far."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I start at nine and I finish at half past five.",
                      "Javi plays video games, but he doesn't study.",
                      "Do you eat at home or at work?",
                      "I go to work by bus because the office is far."
                    ]
                  }
                },
